# The index of `⟨abab⁻¹⟩` in the presented group of `J₁`

The classification list carries the first Janko group as the group `SporadicName.J1.Group`
defined by the transcribed ATLAS presentation

```text
⟨a, b | a², b³, (ab)⁷, (ab(abab⁻¹)³)⁵, (ab(abab⁻¹)⁶abab(ab⁻¹)²)²⟩
```

(`TauCeti.Sporadic.j1Presentation`). This file proves that the cyclic subgroup generated by
`u = abab⁻¹` has finite index at most `9240` in that group. The group `J₁` has order
`175560 = 9240 * 19` and its elements of order `19` generate its largest cyclic subgroups, so
this is the index bound that, with a bound `19` on the order of `u`, shows that the presented
group is no larger than `J₁`.

## The table

The bound is certified by a ranked coset table (`TauCeti.RankedCosetTable`) on `9240` points for
the three letters `a`, `b`, `b⁻¹`, numbered `0`, `1`, `2`. The points are numbered so that the
table needs little data.

* The points `3 * T`, `3 * T + 1`, `3 * T + 2` form a cycle of `b`, so `b` and `b⁻¹` act by a
  formula. The edges `(b, 3 * T)` and `(b, 3 * T + 1)` belong to the forest; `(b, 3 * T + 2)` is
  deduced by the relator `bbb`, and each edge of `b⁻¹` by `b⁻¹b` from its reverse.
* The head `3 * T` of every cycle but the first is the image under `a` of an earlier point, its
  parent in the forest. The point `0` is the root.
* The image under `a` is stored for every point. The `a`-edges that do not belong to the forest
  join two points defined before: one of the two directions of such an edge is deduced by a
  cyclic rotation of one of the three long relators, or, for the edge at the root, by the word
  `abab⁻¹` itself, and the other direction follows by the relator `aa`. These deductions are
  arranged in twenty layers, an edge of one layer using only the forest and the earlier layers;
  the layer of an edge gives its rank.

Each point `j` therefore carries `26` bits: the image of `j` under `a` (`14` bits) and, when
`j` is the smaller endpoint of its `a`-edge, the layer of that edge (`5` bits), a code for the
closed word (`6` bits), and one bit telling which direction the closed word deduces. Fourteen
such fields make one row of the data, and the rows are grouped in ten blocks of `924` points.

The table was computed outside Lean from the action of the permutation model
`TauCeti.Sporadic.J1` on the cosets of the subgroup generated by the image of `abab⁻¹`, which
tells where each generator sends each coset and which relator cycles close. None of this is
trusted: `TauCeti.RankedCosetTable.checkEdge` verifies every edge of the table in the kernel,
block by block, and `TauCeti.RankedCosetTable.index_le` turns the checks into the bound for the
presented group.

In the signed letters of `TauCeti.CosetEnumeration.wordValue`, `0` is `a`, `2` is `b` and `3` is
`b⁻¹`, so `u` is the value of the word `[0, 2, 0, 3]`.

## Main results

* `TauCeti.SporadicName.finiteIndex_zpowers_wordValue_ababInv_group_J1`: the subgroup generated
  by `abab⁻¹` has finite index in the presented group of `J₁`.
* `TauCeti.SporadicName.index_zpowers_wordValue_ababInv_group_J1_le`: its index is at most
  `9240`.

## References

* R. A. Wilson, R. A. Parker, and J. N. Bray, *ATLAS of Finite Group Representations*,
  version 3, `J₁`, section "Presentation",
  <https://brauer.maths.qmul.ac.uk/Atlas/v3/spor/J1/>.
* D. F. Holt, B. Eick and E. A. O'Brien, *Handbook of Computational Group Theory*, Chapman &
  Hall/CRC (2005), Chapter 5, for coset enumeration.
